do is we're essentially  going to take an initial an initial  cloud of data this is kind of  representing a big gouge and cloud with  unit variance 0 mean we're going to  stretch it out into an ellipsoid given  by it's going to be stretched out by a  factor of 2 in this direction and  smashed by a factor of 2 in the other  direction so the singular values are 2  and point 5 and then we're gonna rotate  this up by an angle of PI over 3 so  we're gonna we're going to stretch it  out rotate it by PI over 3 and then move  the center to the to one location inside  of the origin and then from that cloud  of data so let's just share the last  arrow so now we're gonna have this kind  of cloud of data that is not at the  origin and then from this cloud of data  we're gonna compute the SVD and we're  going to show that the SVD recovers kind  of these principal axes of maximum  variance and minimum variance it'll also  give confidence intervals that you can  use to quantify how likely is a new data  point that it belonged to this  distribution very very useful okay  so again all of this is just plotting  this is constructing the X matrix just  how I described we're going to stretch  it out rotate it and add the center to  it and then what we're gonna do is  compute the principal components of that  data matrix and so the the principal  component analysis of that data matrix  the first thing we're gonna do is we're  gonna compute the average kind of the  center of the data and we're going to  subtract that Center from all of the  data so we're gonna bring it back to the  origin then we're gonna compute the  singular value decomposition of that  mean Center data and what it's going to  do is it's going to tell us the rotation  the U matrix that rotates into this this  frame and it's going to determine the  Sigma matrix that'll tell how stretched  our distribution is along its prints  Laxus how how much variance in the first  direction how much variance in the  second direction and so on and so forth  so I'm going to plot these and then  we're also going to figure out what the  one standard deviation confidence  interval and two standard deviation and  three standard deviation are based on  that information so let's just run this  and see what it looks like okay great so  this is the original distribution of  data these are they're 10,000 points in  X 1 and X 2 and notice that the center  of the data is not exactly at 0-0 it's a  2-1 okay so it's not mean centered it  has these directions of variants that  are high variance low variance and it  sits at an angle of PI over three so  when you when you Cape take the SVD of  that matrix of data then what you find  is a sigma that essentially has two  elements that are 2 and 0.5 and that  tells you that the first principal  direction has you know a lot of variance  to a variance of 2 and the second  direction has has small variance of  variance of 0.5 okay what it also tells  you the information in this u matrix  this tells you how this distribution is  rotated at what angle are those  principal components so literally the  red lines here the first line is the  first column of U that's the first  principal component and the second the  second line is the second column that's  the second principal component that  tells you the the direction of maximum  variance and then the direction of the  second direction of a variance in this  case of minimal variance ok and what I  think is really fun is that you can then  use these these actually have  statistical meaning because this is a  pca is built on the assumption that your  data is normally or Gaussian distributed  and our data is because we cooked it up  to be that way and so you can  essentially compute these confidence  intervals  X standard deviation where you take you  times Sigma times a circle so I take a  little unit circle and if I multiply  that unit circle by Sigma and then by  you it maps into this kind of unit  standard deviation ellipse and that's  what I'm plotting as this inner blue  ellipse here the the middle blue ellipse  is a two standard deviation ellipse and  the outer blue ellipse is a three  standard deviation ellipse so this  actually gives you beautiful confidence  intervals so if I have a new data point  out here I can say well that's you know  four or five standard deviations outside  of the distribution it's almost  certainly not on the distribution or as  if I get a data point here I can say how  confident I am that it actually came  from its distribution okay so this was  an example that we cooked up where we  knew the answer for for the principal  components but this just shows that you  can use the SVD to to get these kind of  directions of maximal variance from data  alone okay  thank you

